# Patients

"Our ministers are short of sense and full of funds!" We hear the people of the country say this every day.

The cruelties prevailing in the country fan the display of the ministers' dullness of mind.

The ever-polished motor cars in which they ride and the mansions they enter upon alighting are sufficient accounts of their financial condition.

We thought only that our ministers lacked strength of mind. They say even that was wrong: not only their mental strength but their bodily strength is deficient.

We thought there was a fever in the ministers' minds and that this was why they could offer no sound programmes—why babbling became policy and derangements became law.

"Not only the mind; the body is the same," say our ministers who govern the country.

We thought the ministers suffered from a disease of the mind. But bodily illness too has joined it, and they suffer from both diseases.

The ministers are patients. Therefore, instead of going to Parliament, they frequently go to hospital.

A life free from illness is wealth without deficiency. But our ministers enjoy every kind of wealth except that one.

Ministers are supposed to cure the country's illness, yet they themselves are patients.

They are supposed to supply medicine for the country's disease. Instead, they themselves search for medicine to consume.

Disease, old age, and death do not choose their victims.

They come to a minister and to Mannarsamy the bullock-cart driver alike.

We know this. Why then do we anguish over these ailing ministers? The reason is enough to make the heart burn whenever we think of it.

Illness comes to a minister. We do not wish that it should; we hope it will not. That is not why we grieve.

Even death will come and will not spare a person because he is a minister. We know that. We are the same people who mourned Patel's death.

We know these things are natural and understand that they may happen to anyone.

Yet we grieve. Do you know why?

For the rest of us, illness does not announce itself before arriving.

For these ministers, it does. And whenever they are to travel abroad, an illness arrives and seizes them. While they remain within the country, they do not appear particularly weakened by disease.

Let illness come to ministers. Let it come precisely when they travel abroad. We do not care.

But are ministers people unable to pay for treatment to cure that illness? No, surely one cannot say so.

When we fall ill, do we not buy medicine from the wages we receive? Do workplaces pay separately for our medical treatment?

Although it is necessary to provide such a facility for worker comrades, this government fails to do so properly and leaves them suffering.

Are ministers destitute mendicants? No, no.

Our ministers may be short of sense, but who could say they are short of funds?

This government has spent Rs. 19,654-6-0 on medical assistance for them.

It is when we see such an account presented that our concern grows.

If we continue reading newspapers, this pain doubles.

First comes a report under the heading "The Minister's Health": he has pain in the lower abdomen, frequent nausea and vomiting, yet continues to attend the Secretariat. A doctor says he must receive treatment at a London hospital within a month.

Two weeks later, we see another announcement that the same minister is travelling to attend a conference in London and will take another two months to return.

Let ministers be patients. Let them receive treatment. But the cruelty lies in saying that we must pay for it; we cannot bear that.

Fortunately, ministers travelling abroad receive money only until their illnesses are cured and stop with that, maintaining dignity within limits. That is enough!

Money is already poured out to them merely because they are ministers. Must we also pay them for being patients? Alas!

(*Dravida Nadu*, 12 August 1951)

---NOTES---
The article satirically links ministers' alleged lack of mental judgment with reported physical illness and publicly funded foreign travel. The amount “ரூ.19654 - 6- 0” uses pre-decimal rupees, annas, and pies and is retained in source notation. The final damaged sentence about payment is translated from its clear governing contrast.
